1
00:00:00,128 --> 00:00:03,123
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:03,460 --> 00:00:07,349
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study

3
00:00:07,593 --> 00:00:10,519
A twelve point three, Enthalpy Changes.

4
00:00:11,041 --> 00:00:14,280
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to define

5
00:00:14,362 --> 00:00:18,413
H and delta H, describe energy conservation

6
00:00:18,645 --> 00:00:22,360
in exothermic and endothermic reactions, read

7
00:00:22,511 --> 00:00:26,308
enthalpy diagrams, and explain what a catalyst does

8
00:00:26,482 --> 00:00:27,817
and does not change.

9
00:00:27,991 --> 00:00:32,008
Enthalpy, H, is the energy content of a system

10
00:00:32,101 --> 00:00:33,436
at constant pressure.

11
00:00:33,610 --> 00:00:35,364
It is a state quantity.

12
00:00:35,515 --> 00:00:39,555
On a diagram you compare the reactant level with the product

13
00:00:39,613 --> 00:00:40,020
level.

14
00:00:40,078 --> 00:00:44,211
The enthalpy change, delta H, is H of the products

15
00:00:44,362 --> 00:00:46,544
minus H of the reactants.

16
00:00:46,568 --> 00:00:48,704
It can be positive or negative.

17
00:00:48,773 --> 00:00:52,036
It is commonly quoted in kilojoules per mole.

18
00:00:52,106 --> 00:00:55,437
H and delta H are not interchangeable.

19
00:00:55,553 --> 00:00:58,967
Delta H is not simply the energy of the products.

20
00:00:59,036 --> 00:01:00,638
Energy is conserved.

21
00:01:00,673 --> 00:01:02,937
It is not created or destroyed.

22
00:01:02,960 --> 00:01:06,954
It is transferred between the reacting system and the surroundings.

23
00:01:07,024 --> 00:01:10,693
In an exothermic change, energy is transferred from the

24
00:01:10,751 --> 00:01:12,388
system to the surroundings.

25
00:01:12,910 --> 00:01:16,567
Products have lower enthalpy than reactants, so delta H

26
00:01:16,683 --> 00:01:17,601
is negative.

27
00:01:17,937 --> 00:01:20,132
The surroundings often feel warmer.

28
00:01:20,468 --> 00:01:22,570
Watch the exothermic profile.

29
00:01:22,848 --> 00:01:24,416
Reactants sit higher.

30
00:01:24,590 --> 00:01:25,856
Products sit lower.

31
00:01:26,065 --> 00:01:28,050
The pathway rises to a peak.

32
00:01:28,294 --> 00:01:32,113
Activation energy is the energy from the reactant level to that

33
00:01:32,195 --> 00:01:35,295
peak, not from zero on the y-axis.

34
00:01:35,446 --> 00:01:39,242
Delta H is the downward difference from reactants to products.

35
00:01:39,579 --> 00:01:43,886
In an endothermic change, energy is transferred from the surroundings

36
00:01:44,002 --> 00:01:45,151
into the system.

37
00:01:45,488 --> 00:01:49,540
Products have higher enthalpy than reactants, so delta

38
00:01:49,621 --> 00:01:51,003
H is positive.

39
00:01:51,397 --> 00:01:53,627
The surroundings often feel cooler.

40
00:01:54,079 --> 00:01:56,099
Watch the endothermic profile.

41
00:01:56,494 --> 00:01:57,853
Products sit higher.

42
00:01:58,375 --> 00:02:02,230
Activation energy is still from the reactant level to the peak.

43
00:02:02,439 --> 00:02:04,529
Delta H is the upward difference.

44
00:02:04,772 --> 00:02:08,604
A reaction that transfers forty-five kilojoules to the surroundings

45
00:02:08,673 --> 00:02:11,750
is exothermic, so delta H is negative.

46
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:15,639
An exothermic reaction still needs activation energy.

47
00:02:15,976 --> 00:02:19,657
A negative delta H does not mean the reaction starts with

48
00:02:19,726 --> 00:02:20,527
no barrier.

49
00:02:20,771 --> 00:02:24,393
A catalyst provides an alternative pathway with a lower

50
00:02:24,451 --> 00:02:25,717
activation energy.

51
00:02:26,054 --> 00:02:28,422
Watch both pathways on one diagram.

52
00:02:28,759 --> 00:02:30,663
The catalysed peak is lower.

53
00:02:31,058 --> 00:02:34,727
The reactant enthalpy and the product enthalpy stay the same,

54
00:02:34,936 --> 00:02:36,944
so delta H is unchanged.

55
00:02:37,188 --> 00:02:38,929
Correct the misconceptions.

56
00:02:39,104 --> 00:02:41,240
Exothermic still needs E a.

57
00:02:41,449 --> 00:02:44,734
A catalyst lowers E a, not delta H.

58
00:02:44,978 --> 00:02:47,369
Endothermic does not destroy energy.

59
00:02:47,578 --> 00:02:50,620
Negative delta H does not mean negative energy.

60
00:02:50,829 --> 00:02:53,047
And delta H is not always positive.

61
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:56,855
If a diagram shows an exothermic reaction with products

62
00:02:56,913 --> 00:03:00,198
above reactants, the levels are the wrong way round.

63
00:03:00,477 --> 00:03:04,471
If an endothermic reaction is labelled delta H less than zero,

64
00:03:04,587 --> 00:03:05,609
the sign is wrong.

65
00:03:06,224 --> 00:03:10,183
If a catalyst is drawn with a lower product enthalpy, only

66
00:03:10,241 --> 00:03:11,669
the peak should have changed.

67
00:03:12,284 --> 00:03:14,281
Examination-style question.

68
00:03:14,990 --> 00:03:15,802
Part a.

69
00:03:16,081 --> 00:03:17,439
Define delta H.

70
00:03:17,613 --> 00:03:18,473
Two marks.

71
00:03:18,682 --> 00:03:22,409
Delta H is the change in enthalpy between products and

72
00:03:22,478 --> 00:03:23,221
reactants.

73
00:03:23,674 --> 00:03:27,680
Delta H equals H products minus H reactants.

74
00:03:28,121 --> 00:03:28,922
Part b.

75
00:03:29,363 --> 00:03:33,496
How does an exothermic enthalpy diagram show the energy change?

76
00:03:34,100 --> 00:03:34,866
Two marks.

77
00:03:35,319 --> 00:03:38,105
Products are lower in enthalpy than reactants.

78
00:03:38,628 --> 00:03:42,853
Energy is transferred to the surroundings, so the difference is negative.

79
00:03:43,004 --> 00:03:43,759
Part c.

80
00:03:43,933 --> 00:03:46,348
How does an endothermic change appear?

81
00:03:46,627 --> 00:03:47,428
Two marks.

82
00:03:47,579 --> 00:03:49,285
Delta H is positive.

83
00:03:49,622 --> 00:03:53,082
Energy is transferred from the surroundings into the system.

84
00:03:53,418 --> 00:03:54,185
Part d.

85
00:03:54,463 --> 00:03:57,424
Explain the effect of a catalyst on the profile.

86
00:03:57,482 --> 00:03:58,249
Three marks.

87
00:03:58,423 --> 00:04:01,023
The catalyst provides a lower E a pathway.

88
00:04:01,360 --> 00:04:04,994
Reactant and product levels are unchanged, so delta H

89
00:04:05,110 --> 00:04:06,062
is unchanged.

90
00:04:06,399 --> 00:04:08,535
Before we finish, complete the model.

91
00:04:08,872 --> 00:04:11,333
H is the energy content of a state.

92
00:04:11,577 --> 00:04:14,375
Delta H is the change between states.

93
00:04:14,538 --> 00:04:17,011
Exothermic, delta H negative.

94
00:04:17,278 --> 00:04:19,774
Endothermic, delta H positive.

95
00:04:19,971 --> 00:04:23,721
A catalyst changes the pathway peak, not reactant

96
00:04:23,791 --> 00:04:26,403
H, product H, or delta H.

97
00:04:26,658 --> 00:04:30,315
If you found this useful, please subscribe to J D Science,

98
00:04:30,513 --> 00:04:33,845
and visit j d science dot co dot u k for more

99
